# Gateaux / Kahawai API — proposed v2 (REST-conventional)

A redesign of the job-runner HTTP surface (gorbachevd) so headless tooling
(`baker.r`, `joblist.r`, CI pipelines) can drive jobs end-to-end with one
auth model, standard verbs, and predictable responses.

Pain points motivating each change come from real PRs/sessions and are
called out inline as "**Why**".

---

## 1. Two auth tiers, one scheme

**Today**: per-report trigger tokens live in URL paths (`/job/<report>/<token>`);
user-scoped JWTs live in `Authorization: Bearer …`. Logs/outputs only accept
the latter, so headless CI either needs a personal JWT or can't pull logs.

**Proposed**: Bearer JWTs *everywhere*, in `Authorization: Bearer <token>`.
Two token tiers — both delivered as Bearer JWTs under the same scheme,
but with different capabilities:

### User tokens (issued via SSO)

Identity-bearing. The token is associated with a specific user account
and carries that user's organisational role + report memberships. Required
for any operation outside a single report's sandbox:

- create / delete / rename reports
- update a report's secrets, env defaults, allowed images, resource caps
- mint / rotate / revoke service-account tokens for a report
- view audit logs across reports
- view your own session list

See §13 for the SSO / device-code flow.

### Service-account tokens (issued by a report owner)

Scoped to one report. Configurable capability set:

| Scope             | What it allows                                            |
|-------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------|
| `jobs:submit`     | POST new jobs                                             |
| `jobs:read`       | GET jobs + job metadata, list, search                     |
| `jobs:cancel`     | DELETE running/pending jobs                               |
| `logs:read`       | GET job logs (incl. SSE tail)                             |
| `outputs:read`    | List + download output files                              |
| `secrets:read`    | View (not modify) the report's env defaults & secret keys |

A token issued with `jobs:submit + jobs:read + jobs:cancel + logs:read +
outputs:read` is "headless CI mode" — replaces today's `.jwt` and adds
all the things we couldn't do with it (cancel, fetch logs/outputs).
Rotatable, revocable, never in URL paths.

> **Why**: removes the "you can submit but can't pull logs" wall, lets
> us grant CI exactly what it needs, and cleanly separates "I'm an
> organisation user" (can administer) from "I'm a CI bot" (can run jobs).

---

## 2. Standard resource layout

```
/api/v2/reports                                             # collection (list/create)
/api/v2/reports/{report}                                    # one report (read/update/delete)
/api/v2/reports/{report}/secrets                            # secret values (write-only echo)
/api/v2/reports/{report}/env                                # env-var defaults
/api/v2/reports/{report}/tokens                             # service-account token mgmt
/api/v2/reports/{report}/jobs                               # job collection
/api/v2/reports/{report}/jobs/{job_id}                      # one job
/api/v2/reports/{report}/jobs/{job_id}/log                  # plain-text log
/api/v2/reports/{report}/jobs/{job_id}/log?stream=true      # SSE log stream
/api/v2/reports/{report}/jobs/{job_id}/outputs              # output listing
/api/v2/reports/{report}/jobs/{job_id}/outputs/{filename}   # one output
/api/v2/reports/{report}/jobs/{job_id}/events               # SSE state stream
/api/v2/auth/device                                         # CLI device-code flow (§13)
/api/v2/auth/sso/callback                                   # OIDC redirect target (§13)
/api/v2/auth/session                                        # current session info
/api/v2/openapi.json                                        # machine-readable spec
```

`/api/v2/` prefix keeps the old `/jobs/<report>/<token>` routes alive during
migration.

---

## 2a. Report resources — create, update, secrets

All routes in this section require a **user token**. Service-account
tokens never reach them.

### List / create reports

```
GET  /api/v2/reports                       # reports I can access
POST /api/v2/reports
  {
    "name":          "DAT2023-04C-models",
    "title":         "PSC models",
    "git": {
      "url":         "git@github.com:kahawai-collective/psc-estimation",
      "subdirectory": "models",
      "branch":      "main"
    },
    "docker":        "ubuntu/psc-estimation",
    "resources":     { "cpus": 4, "memory": 8 },
    "wants":         ["DAT2023-04C-gather", "DAT2023-04C-report"]
  }
→ 201 Created
   Location: /api/v2/reports/DAT2023-04C-models
```

### Read / update / delete

```
GET    /api/v2/reports/{report}
PATCH  /api/v2/reports/{report}     # partial update; same shape as POST
DELETE /api/v2/reports/{report}     # archives report + its job history
```

### Secrets

Secrets are write-only via the API — once stored they are never echoed
back as cleartext. The collection endpoint returns only key names.

```
GET  /api/v2/reports/{report}/secrets
→ { "keys": ["AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID", "AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY"] }

PUT  /api/v2/reports/{report}/secrets/{key}
  { "value": "AKIA..." }
→ 204 No Content

DELETE /api/v2/reports/{report}/secrets/{key}
→ 204 No Content
```

### Env defaults (non-secret)

Values *are* readable. Used as a fallback when a job submission doesn't
override a given key.

```
GET  /api/v2/reports/{report}/env
→ { "AWS_DATA_BUCKET": "s3://psc-estimation/2023-24", "BUILD_DATE": "2026-05-25" }

PUT  /api/v2/reports/{report}/env/{key}
  { "value": "..." }
→ 204 No Content
```

### Service-account tokens

```
GET  /api/v2/reports/{report}/tokens
→ [ { "id": "tok_abc", "name": "ci-baker",
      "scopes": ["jobs:submit","jobs:read","logs:read","outputs:read"],
      "created_at": "...", "last_used_at": "..." } ]

POST /api/v2/reports/{report}/tokens
  { "name": "ci-baker",
    "scopes": ["jobs:submit","jobs:read","logs:read","outputs:read"] }
→ 201 Created
   { "id": "tok_abc",
     "token": "kah_v2_…",       # shown ONCE, never again
     "scopes": [...] }

DELETE /api/v2/reports/{report}/tokens/{id}    # revoke
```

> **Why** (user-tier requirement): creating a report or rotating a
> report's secrets must trace back to a human; a service account
> can't bootstrap itself.

---

## 3. Verbs follow REST conventions

| Verb     | Path                                          | Action                                |
|----------|-----------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------|
| `POST`   | `/api/v2/reports/{report}/jobs`               | Submit a new job                      |
| `GET`    | `/api/v2/reports/{report}/jobs`               | List (with filters + pagination)      |
| `GET`    | `/api/v2/reports/{report}/jobs/{job_id}`      | Read one job                          |
| `DELETE` | `/api/v2/reports/{report}/jobs/{job_id}`      | Cancel a running/pending job          |
| `GET`    | `/api/v2/reports/{report}/jobs/{job_id}/log`  | Fetch log (with `?since=<ts>`)         |
| `GET`    | `/api/v2/reports/{report}/jobs/{job_id}/outputs`            | List output files       |
| `GET`    | `/api/v2/reports/{report}/jobs/{job_id}/outputs/{filename}` | Download one file       |

> **Why**: PUT is currently used for what is semantically a POST (creating
> a new resource). RESTful PUT is idempotent replacement of a known URI;
> POST is for collection-append. Today's PUT has neither idempotency nor
> a known URI.

---

## 4. Submission body — clean separation

**Today (kahawai trigger)**:
```json
{ "TAG": "x", "PREDICTOR_TAG": "y", "SAMPLE_PRIOR": "only" }
```
Env vars flat at the top. Easy to typo `"env": {…}` and get a silent failure.

**Today (gateaux)**:
```json
{ "cpus": 4, "memory": 8, "env": {...}, "wants": [...], "requires": [...] }
```
Job metadata mixed with env. Inconsistent with the kahawai shape.

**Proposed**:
```json
{
  "tag":       "scornful-porcupine",
  "env":       { "PREDICTOR_TAG": "bycatch-dfffs", "SAMPLE_PRIOR": "yes" },
  "resources": { "cpus": 4, "memory": 8, "gpus": null },
  "deps": {
    "wants":    [{"report": "DAT2023-04C-psc-estimates-db", "job_id": 151023}],
    "requires": [{"report": "DAT2023-04C-psc-estimates-db"}]
  },
  "webhook":   "https://my.ci/hook"
}
```

Rules:
- `tag` is a top-level first-class field, not abused as `env.TAG`.
- `env` is *always* a JSON object; flat strings or top-level env keys
  return **400 Bad Request** with a clear error.
- `deps` separates `wants` (best-effort version pin) from `requires`
  (hard pre-requisite).
- `resources` overrides the report's `kahawai.yaml` defaults.
- Optional `webhook` URL — server POSTs job state transitions to it (see §7).

> **Why**: every silent failure we hit this session ("env" wrapper bug,
> tag-as-env, etc.) becomes a loud 400 with a JSON error pointing at the
> field. Schema mismatches surface at submit time, not in the container.

---

## 5. Submission response

**Today**: `{"result": "Processed"}` — no job id. We re-fetch the joblist
filtered by TAG to recover the id.

**Proposed**: `201 Created` with `Location: /api/v2/reports/{report}/jobs/151045`
and a body that includes everything we'd want to audit later:

```json
{
  "id":           151045,
  "report":       "DAT2023-04C-models",
  "tag":          "scornful-porcupine",
  "status":       "pending",
  "created_at":   "2026-05-29T11:09:09Z",
  "started_at":   null,
  "finished_at":  null,
  "actor": {
    "type":       "service_account",
    "token_id":   "tok_abc",
    "token_name": "ci-baker",
    "user":       null
  },
  "git": {
    "url":          "git@github.com:kahawai-collective/psc-estimation",
    "branch":       "main",
    "commit_sha":   "a1b2c3d4...",
    "submodules":   [
      { "path": "lib/bakeR",      "commit": "fd317d6..." },
      { "path": "bibliography",   "commit": "4d1a38e..." }
    ]
  },
  "image": {
    "ref":          "ubuntu/psc-estimation:latest",
    "digest":       "sha256:f14730c6..."
  },
  "env":          { "PREDICTOR_TAG": "bycatch-dfffs", "ITER": "2000" },
  "env_resolved": { "PREDICTOR_TAG": "bycatch-dfffs", "ITER": "2000",
                    "AWS_DATA_BUCKET": "s3://psc-estimation/2023-24",
                    "BUILD_DATE": "2026-05-25" },
  "resources":    { "cpus": 4, "memory": 8 },
  "deps": {
    "requires":  [{ "report": "DAT2023-04C-psc-estimates-db", "job_id": 151023 }]
  },
  "apparatchik":  null,
  "links": {
    "self":    "/api/v2/reports/DAT2023-04C-models/jobs/151045",
    "log":     "/api/v2/reports/DAT2023-04C-models/jobs/151045/log",
    "outputs": "/api/v2/reports/DAT2023-04C-models/jobs/151045/outputs",
    "events":  "/api/v2/reports/DAT2023-04C-models/jobs/151045/events"
  }
}
```

Key audit fields:
- **`actor`** — who/what submitted (user account or service-account token id).
- **`git`** — exact commit + submodule pins the apparatchik checked out.
- **`image`** — docker ref + content-addressable digest (so a `:latest`
  tag can still be reproduced).
- **`env`** vs **`env_resolved`** — what the caller asked for vs the
  full set actually exported to the container (after merging report
  env defaults).

> **Why**: caller knows the id immediately, *and* can later reconstruct
> exactly what code/env/image produced any given job. No more "which
> commit was 151044 running again?"

> **Why**: caller knows the id immediately. No racey TAG-match polling.
> `links` is HATEOAS-lite — clients can follow them without hardcoding
> URL templates.

---

## 6. Listing and search — server-side filters + pagination

Comma-separated multi-values, glob patterns, and time ranges all supported.
All filters AND together; use multiple values within one filter for OR.

```
GET /api/v2/reports/{report}/jobs
    ?status=running,pending                        # OR within status
    &tag=scornful-porcupine                        # exact match
    &env.PREDICTOR_TAG=bycatch-*                   # glob on any env key
    &git.commit_sha=a1b2c3d4*                      # commit prefix
    &actor.user=edward@dragonfly.co.nz             # who submitted
    &created_after=2026-05-29T00:00:00Z
    &created_before=2026-05-30T00:00:00Z
    &finished=true                                 # has finished_at
    &page=1&per_page=50
    &sort=-id                                      # -created_at, duration, …
    &fields=id,status,tag,env.PREDICTOR_TAG        # sparse fieldsets
```

Cross-report search (user token only):

```
GET /api/v2/jobs?report=DAT2023-04C-*&status=failure&created_after=...
```

Response headers: `X-Total-Count`, `X-Page`, `X-Per-Page`, `Link`
(RFC 5988 next/prev). Body: `{"results": [...]}`.

ETags + `If-None-Match` for cheap polling — server returns 304 if no
jobs matching the query have changed since last poll.

> **Why**: today we GET the entire joblist (14 KB and growing) every
> poll. Server-side filter + ETag + sparse fields = constant-time
> polling that also lets us answer "which bycatch jobs failed in the
> last hour" without grepping locally.

---

## 7. Status transitions — push, don't poll

Three complementary mechanisms:

- **Long-poll**:
  `GET /api/v2/reports/{report}/jobs/{job_id}?wait=30`
  blocks up to 30 s; returns immediately if status changes.

- **SSE event stream**:
  `GET /api/v2/reports/{report}/jobs/{job_id}/events` with
  `Accept: text/event-stream`. Emits `pending → running → success` etc.

- **Webhook**: caller passes `webhook` URL at submit time; server POSTs
  state transitions to it. Includes a signature header
  (`X-Kahawai-Signature: sha256=…`) keyed off the service-account token.

> **Why**: today, watching 16 jobs means re-pulling the full joblist on
> a timer. With SSE or webhooks the caller does no polling at all.

---

## 8. Logs and outputs — first-class

```
GET /api/v2/reports/{report}/jobs/{job_id}/log
    ?since=<timestamp>          # incremental (returns lines >= ts)
    Accept: text/plain          # default
    Accept: text/event-stream   # live tail (SSE)

GET /api/v2/reports/{report}/jobs/{job_id}/outputs
→ {
    "files": [
      { "name": "model.rdata", "size": 2734291,
        "mime": "application/x-rdata",
        "url":  "/api/v2/reports/DAT.../jobs/151045/outputs/model.rdata",
        "checksum": "sha256:…" }
    ]
  }
```

A range request (`Range: bytes=…`) works for both log and output files —
useful for resumable downloads of large model artefacts.

> **Why**: this is the gap that broke headless log retrieval for us. The
> route exists in dev (gorbachevd); it just needs to honour the
> service-account token under the new auth model.

---

## 9. Cancel

```
DELETE /api/v2/reports/{report}/jobs/{job_id}
→ 202 Accepted   # cancellation initiated; not yet stopped
   { "id": 151045, "status": "cancelling" }
   
GET /api/v2/reports/{report}/jobs/151045
→ { "status": "cancelled", "cancelled_at": "..." }
```

Idempotent — DELETE on an already-cancelled or already-finished job
returns 200 OK with current state, not an error.

> **Why**: `cancel_initiated_at` is already in the job model; we just
> never had a public route to set it from a script.

---

## 10. Idempotency

Optional header `Idempotency-Key: <uuid>` on POST: server stores
key→job_id mapping for 24 h. Re-submitting the same key returns the
existing job's 201 response. Caller can safely retry on network blips.

> **Why**: today, a network blip on PUT either creates a duplicate or
> silently fails — caller has no way to distinguish.

---

## 11. Error responses

All errors return a JSON body of the form:

```json
{
  "error": {
    "code":    "INVALID_ENV_SHAPE",
    "message": "env must be a JSON object; got string",
    "details": { "field": "env", "received_type": "string" },
    "trace_id": "..."
  }
}
```

Status code matrix:

| Code | When                                                                            |
|------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| 400  | Malformed JSON, wrong types, missing required fields                            |
| 401  | Missing or expired token                                                        |
| 403  | Valid token but lacks the scope (e.g. `jobs:cancel`) or no access to the report |
| 404  | Unknown report or job                                                           |
| 409  | State conflict (e.g. cancel on already-finished job — though we make this 200)  |
| 422  | Semantically invalid (unknown predictor_tag, dep cycle, etc.)                   |
| 429  | Rate-limited; `Retry-After` header                                              |

---

## 12. OpenAPI spec

The server exposes `/api/v2/openapi.json` (and `.yaml`). bakeR's R helpers
can be auto-generated from it via `rapiclient` or similar.

> **Why**: today the API shape lives in (a) the Haskell source, (b) the
> R helpers, and (c) people's heads. A single machine-readable schema
> kills the drift and is the foundation for a Claude skill (separate
> task in memory).

---

## 13. Auth architecture: Keycloak + multi-IdP

We use **Keycloak** as the identity broker, with **GitHub** and
**Google** as the primary external identity providers. The architecture
is extensible to others (Microsoft Entra, GitLab, ORCID, institutional
SAML) without code change — just Keycloak admin configuration.

```
                   ┌─────────────────────────────────┐
                   │       Identity Providers        │
                   │                                 │
                   │   GitHub   Google   (others…)   │
                   └─────┬───────────┬───────────────┘
                         │ OAuth 2.0 / OIDC
                         ▼
                   ┌─────────────────────────────────┐
                   │            Keycloak             │
                   │  realm: dragonfly               │
                   │  ├── identity-providers/        │  ← brokers external IdPs
                   │  ├── clients/                   │  ← our apps register here
                   │  │   ├── kahawai-web   (public) │
                   │  │   ├── kahawai-cli   (public) │
                   │  │   └── gorbachevd-server      │
                   │  └── users/  (federated)        │
                   └─────┬───────────────────────────┘
                         │ id_token + access_token (JWT)
                         ▼
                   ┌─────────────────────────────────┐
                   │      gorbachevd (the API)       │
                   │  • verifies Keycloak JWTs       │
                   │  • mints its own service-       │
                   │    account JWTs (separate key)  │
                   │  • per-report ACL in DB         │
                   └─────────────────────────────────┘
```

Two distinct token-issuance paths share one validation layer:

- **User tokens** (interactive logins) — minted by Keycloak after a user
  authenticates via GitHub/Google/etc.
- **Service-account tokens** (per-report, `kah_v2_…`) — minted by
  gorbachevd itself, signed with its own key. Don't go through Keycloak.

The API distinguishes them by JWT `iss`:
- `iss = https://kc.dragonfly.co.nz/realms/dragonfly` → user token,
  validate against Keycloak JWKS.
- `iss = https://api.kahawai.io` → service-account, validate against
  gorbachevd's own key.

---

## 14. Keycloak setup

### 14.1 Realm

One realm: `dragonfly`. Holds all of:
- The IdP brokers (GitHub, Google, …)
- The client registrations (web, CLI, server)
- Federated users (one Keycloak user per real person, even if they log
  in via multiple external IdPs — see §16 on account linking)
- Realm roles (`kahawai-admin`, `kahawai-user`, …)

### 14.2 Clients

Three Keycloak clients, with different settings:

| Client                | Type    | Access type   | Flows enabled                             | Notes                              |
|-----------------------|---------|---------------|-------------------------------------------|------------------------------------|
| `kahawai-web`         | OpenID  | public        | Standard (auth code + PKCE)               | Browser web UI                     |
| `kahawai-cli`         | OpenID  | public        | Device authorization grant (RFC 8628)     | R/CLI scripts, no client secret    |
| `gorbachevd-server`   | OpenID  | bearer-only   | none                                      | Backend; just validates tokens     |

Important client settings:

- **`kahawai-web`** —
  - Valid Redirect URIs:
    `https://kahawai.io/api/v2/auth/sso/callback`,
    `https://gateaux.io/api/v2/auth/sso/callback`
  - Web Origins: same (for CORS)
  - PKCE Required: S256
  - Implicit Flow: **disabled** (use auth code + PKCE only)
- **`kahawai-cli`** —
  - "OAuth 2.0 Device Authorization Grant Enabled": ON
  - No client secret (public client)
  - Token lifetimes: access token 15 min, refresh token 30 days
- **`gorbachevd-server`** —
  - "Bearer-only": ON — Keycloak refuses to *issue* tokens to this
    client; it only validates ones presented *to* it.

### 14.3 Realm roles

Two coarse roles at the realm level:

- `kahawai-admin` — can create reports, manage all reports, manage
  realm-level settings (e.g. registering new IdPs at the app level).
- `kahawai-user` — baseline gate. Can be granted access to specific
  reports via application-level ACL (§17).

Fine-grained per-report permissions live in gorbachevd's DB. Keycloak
only tells us "who"; gorbachevd decides "what they can do where". This
keeps report-membership churn out of Keycloak.

---

## 15. Identity Provider configuration

### 15.1 GitHub

1. Create an OAuth App at <https://github.com/settings/developers>
   (or a GitHub App for richer org-membership lookups — see §15.1.1):
   - **Homepage URL**: `https://kahawai.io`
   - **Authorization callback URL**:
     `https://kc.dragonfly.co.nz/realms/dragonfly/broker/github/endpoint`
   - Note the **Client ID** and **Client Secret**.
2. In Keycloak: **Identity Providers → Add provider → GitHub**:
   - Alias: `github`
   - Display name: `Sign in with GitHub`
   - Client ID / Client Secret: paste from step 1
   - Default scopes: `user:email read:org` (the latter only if we want
     org-membership info)
   - **Trust Email**: ON (verified-GitHub-email users auto-link to an
     existing Keycloak user with the same email — §16)
   - **Store tokens**: ON (lets the app call back into the GitHub API
     as the user, e.g. to verify org membership at submit time)
3. **Mappers** (Keycloak → JWT claims): default GitHub mappers cover
   `email`, `preferred_username`, `name`. For organisation membership,
   add a custom mapper (see §15.1.1) and emit a `github_orgs` claim.

#### 15.1.1 Org membership

GitHub's basic IdP mapper doesn't include organisation membership. To
enforce "only members of the `dragonfly-science` org can log in":

- **Easy**: a Keycloak Authenticator extension that, on first login,
  calls `GET https://api.github.com/user/orgs` with the stored token
  and denies the login if `dragonfly-science` isn't present.
- **Cleaner**: deploy `keycloak-github-org-mapper` (or similar) — emits
  a `github_orgs` claim, then a Keycloak Authentication Flow
  conditional rejects if the claim doesn't include the required org.
- **Skip**: don't enforce in Keycloak; let any GitHub user in.
  Authorisation is then entirely in gorbachevd's per-report ACL.
  Simplest, and probably fine for v1.

### 15.2 Google

1. In Google Cloud Console → **APIs & Services → Credentials → Create
   credentials → OAuth client ID**:
   - Application type: Web application
   - Authorised redirect URIs:
     `https://kc.dragonfly.co.nz/realms/dragonfly/broker/google/endpoint`
   - Note the **Client ID** and **Client Secret**.
2. In Keycloak: **Identity Providers → Add provider → Google**:
   - Alias: `google`
   - Client ID / Client Secret: paste
   - Default scopes: `openid email profile`
   - **Trust Email**: ON
   - **Hosted Domain**: optionally restrict to `dragonfly.co.nz` — this
     passes `hd=dragonfly.co.nz` to Google so only that Workspace can
     sign in. Caveat: blocks external collaborators.
3. **Mappers**: default Google mappers cover `email`, `name`,
   `given_name`, `family_name`. Workspace group membership requires
   Google Directory API access (separate Workspace admin step).

### 15.3 Adding more IdPs later

The pattern repeats: register an OAuth/OIDC client with the new IdP,
point its callback at Keycloak's broker endpoint, paste credentials into
Keycloak's "Add identity provider" form, configure mappers.

The user-facing experience is "the login page shows a button per IdP".
Adding a button is a Keycloak admin task; **no code change in the API
or the clients is needed**.

---

## 16. Account linking and multi-IdP

A user might have a GitHub account *and* a Google account, both with
the same verified email. We want them to be **the same Keycloak user**,
so they can log in via either path tomorrow without losing access to
reports they own today.

Keycloak's built-in **First Broker Login flow** handles this. Default
behaviour (recommended):

- On first login via an IdP, Keycloak looks for an existing user with
  the same verified email.
- If found, prompts the new user to confirm: *"Link this GitHub account
  to existing user edward@dragonfly.co.nz?"* After re-authenticating
  the existing path (e.g. logging in once with Google), the two
  external identities are linked to one Keycloak user.
- If not found, a new Keycloak user is created.

Tweak the **Trust Email** setting per-IdP: if an IdP's email is
unverified, don't auto-link. Both GitHub and Google return verified
emails by default for properly set-up users.

---

## 17. Token validation and authorisation in gorbachevd

### 17.1 Validation middleware

A Bearer-token middleware does:

1. Parse JWT header → read `kid` and `iss`.
2. Branch:
   - `iss == "https://kc.dragonfly.co.nz/realms/dragonfly"`:
     fetch JWKS from `${iss}/protocol/openid-connect/certs` (cache
     ~1h; refresh on `kid` miss). Verify signature.
   - `iss == "https://api.kahawai.io"`:
     verify against gorbachevd's own signing key.
3. Common checks: `exp`, `nbf`, `aud` (must include `kahawai-web`,
   `kahawai-cli`, or `gorbachevd-server`), `iss` (as above).
4. Build a `Principal`:

   ```haskell
   data Principal
     = UserPrincipal           -- minted by Keycloak
         { subject    :: Text     -- 'sub' claim, stable user id
         , email      :: Text
         , name       :: Text
         , idp        :: Text     -- 'github' / 'google' / …
         , realmRoles :: [Text]
         }
     | ServicePrincipal        -- minted by gorbachevd
         { tokenId   :: Text
         , tokenName :: Text
         , reportId  :: Text
         , scopes    :: [Scope]
         }
   ```

### 17.2 Per-route policy

- **User-only routes** (`Auth` / `Reports` create-update-delete /
  `Secrets` / `Tokens` / cross-report `GET /jobs`) — service-account
  tokens get 403.
- **Mixed routes** (`Jobs` / `Logs` / `Outputs` / `Events`) — either
  principal works:
  - A `ServicePrincipal` must have a matching `reportId` and the right
    scope.
  - A `UserPrincipal` must be a member of the report (looked up in
    gorbachevd's `report_member` table).

### 17.3 Per-report ACL

```
report_member
  report_id   text
  user_sub    text     -- = Keycloak 'sub' claim
  role        text     -- 'owner' | 'admin' | 'viewer'
  PRIMARY KEY (report_id, user_sub)
```

- `'owner'` is what `POST /reports/{report}/tokens`,
  `PATCH /reports/{report}`, `DELETE /reports/{report}` require.
- `'admin'` covers everything except deletion + ownership transfer.
- `'viewer'` allows GET on jobs/logs/outputs but not submit/cancel.

Realm role `kahawai-admin` is a global override (can act on any
report). Realm role `kahawai-user` is the baseline gate — without it,
the app refuses login even if the Keycloak token is valid.

### 17.4 User onboarding

- The first user (bootstrap admin) is granted `kahawai-admin` in
  Keycloak directly.
- Subsequent users self-register via SSO. By default they get
  `kahawai-user`; report owners then invite them into specific reports.

### 17.5 Haskell libraries

`jose-jwt` or `crypton-jwt` for parsing/verification; `http-conduit`
for JWKS fetch; an LRU cache (e.g. `lrucaching`) keyed by `kid` for
verified keys.

---

## 18. End-to-end auth flows

Three flows on top of the Keycloak architecture — pick by context.

### 18.1 Browser SSO (web UI)

1. User clicks "Sign in" → app sends 302 to
   `https://kc.dragonfly.co.nz/realms/dragonfly/protocol/openid-connect/auth?
     client_id=kahawai-web
    &redirect_uri=https://kahawai.io/api/v2/auth/sso/callback
    &response_type=code
    &code_challenge=…&code_challenge_method=S256
    &scope=openid email profile
    &state=<csrf>`
2. Keycloak shows the IdP chooser ("Sign in with GitHub", "Sign in
   with Google"). User picks one.
3. Keycloak redirects to that IdP. User authenticates there.
4. IdP returns to Keycloak's broker endpoint
   (`…/realms/dragonfly/broker/<alias>/endpoint`). Keycloak resolves
   or creates a federated user (§16), then redirects back to the app's
   callback with a `code`.
5. App exchanges the `code` for tokens via Keycloak's token endpoint
   (client_id=kahawai-web + PKCE verifier). Receives `id_token`,
   `access_token`, `refresh_token`.
6. App sets `Set-Cookie: kah_session=…; HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Lax`
   and surfaces a `GET /api/v2/auth/session` endpoint that returns the
   current access token for paste-into-CLI use.

API summary:
```
GET  /api/v2/auth/sso/login?redirect=<app_url>     → 302 to Keycloak
GET  /api/v2/auth/sso/callback?code=…&state=…      → 302 to redirect; sets cookie
GET  /api/v2/auth/session                          → { user, expires_at, bearer }
```

### 18.2 Device-code (CLI / R from a laptop)

OAuth 2.0 device authorization grant (RFC 8628), proxied to Keycloak's
device-code endpoints.

1. CLI calls `POST /api/v2/auth/device` (proxy to Keycloak's
   `/realms/dragonfly/protocol/openid-connect/auth/device` with
   `client_id=kahawai-cli`). Keycloak returns a `device_code` and
   `user_code`.
2. CLI displays *"Open https://kc.dragonfly.co.nz/realms/dragonfly/device
   and enter WDJB-MJHT"*.
3. User opens the URL in a browser, picks GitHub or Google, approves.
4. CLI polls `POST /api/v2/auth/device/token` (proxied to Keycloak's
   token endpoint with `grant_type=urn:ietf:params:oauth:grant-type:device_code`)
   until it gets the bearer.
5. CLI writes the token to `~/.config/kahawai/token` (or `KAHAWAI_TOKEN`
   env). Subsequent calls use `Authorization: Bearer …`.
6. On expiry: refresh transparently using the stored refresh token
   (Keycloak supports refresh for public clients with `offline_access`
   scope).

```
POST /api/v2/auth/device
→ { "device_code": "…", "user_code": "WDJB-MJHT",
    "verification_url": "…", "interval": 5, "expires_in": 600 }

POST /api/v2/auth/device/token   { "device_code": "…" }
→ 400 { "error": { "code": "AUTHORIZATION_PENDING" } }   # keep polling
→ 200 { "bearer": "kah_v2_user_…", "expires_at": "..." }  # done
```

### 18.3 Service-account (non-interactive)

1. Logged-in user (holding a Keycloak token, via 18.1 or 18.2) calls
   `POST /api/v2/reports/{report}/tokens` with `name + scopes`.
2. gorbachevd mints a JWT signed with its own key. Returns it once.
3. The CI runner stores it as a secret env var. All subsequent
   `Authorization: Bearer …` calls validate against gorbachevd's own
   key (no Keycloak involvement on the validation path).
4. Revocation: `DELETE /api/v2/reports/{report}/tokens/{id}` flips a
   row in `service_account_tokens.revoked_at`; the middleware checks
   this on every request.

### 18.4 Session management

```
GET    /api/v2/auth/sessions          # list my active sessions
DELETE /api/v2/auth/sessions/{id}     # revoke one
DELETE /api/v2/auth/sessions          # revoke all (logout everywhere)
```

> **Why** (the whole §13–18 stack): today you log into the web UI and
> there's no obvious path from "I'm a logged-in user" to "I have a JWT
> I can paste into a script". Device-code flow is the standard OAuth
> way to bridge that gap — it's how `gh auth login`, `gcloud auth
> login`, `kubectl oidc-login` etc. work. And by routing through
> Keycloak, adding new IdPs is an admin task, not a code change.

---

## 19. Operational concerns

### 19.1 Keycloak deployment

- Deploy with a **stateful database** (Postgres). Not the H2/dev DB.
- Backups of Keycloak's DB are essential — losing it means everyone
  re-registers and re-grants.
- Behind a TLS-terminating reverse proxy (caddy, nginx) at a stable
  URL — the `iss` claim is baked into JWTs, so changing the URL is a
  breaking change.

### 19.2 JWKS rotation

- Keycloak rotates signing keys on its own schedule. gorbachevd must
  re-fetch JWKS when it sees an unknown `kid` (and on a 1h timer for
  general freshness).
- Old keys remain in the JWKS for a grace period so existing tokens
  validate until expiry.

### 19.3 IdP credential rotation

- GitHub and Google client secrets in Keycloak are themselves
  rotatable. Schedule a yearly rotation.
- Store live values in Keycloak's secret storage (encrypted); they
  appear in realm exports as `${vault.…}` placeholders.

### 19.4 Local dev

- A `docker-compose` for Keycloak + Postgres in
  `dragonfly/gorbachevd/` is the cleanest path. Pre-seed the realm
  with `kahawai-web`, `kahawai-cli`, `gorbachevd-server` clients and
  a couple of test users.
- For dev, mock IdPs (or use Keycloak's local login without external
  IdPs) — registering real GitHub/Google clients per developer is
  friction.

### 19.5 Troubleshooting

- `kcadm.sh` is the CLI for Keycloak —
  `kcadm.sh get realms/dragonfly/identity-provider/instances` inspects
  IdPs; `kcadm.sh get events` reads the audit log.
- gorbachevd should log the `sub` + `iss` for every accepted request
  (not the token!) to make access traces easy.
- Common failures:
  - "Email already exists" on first GitHub login → email clash with
    an existing user; the First Broker Login flow's reconciliation
    prompt handles this if the user clicks through. Set the flow to
    **Auto-Link** if you trust both IdPs' email verification.
  - 401 on an old token → probable JWKS rotation; bump the JWKS
    cache TTL down or force refresh.

---

## Migration sketch

Two parallel tracks — the auth track (Keycloak) and the API track
(v2 routes) can be developed in lockstep but rolled out independently
since the v2 routes accept *either* a Keycloak token or a
service-account token.

### Auth track

A0. Stand up Keycloak in dev with a single test IdP (Keycloak's own
    local login). gorbachevd validates Keycloak JWTs alongside its
    existing auth.

A1. Wire up Google IdP first (Workspace domain restriction gives free
    org-membership gating). Internal users log in via Google.

A2. Wire up GitHub IdP. Decide on org-membership policy (§15.1.1).

A3. Implement service-account token issuance + the per-report ACL DB
    layer. Migrate existing `.jwt` trigger tokens to the new
    service-account tokens; deprecate the URL-path-token routes.

A4. Open to additional IdPs (Microsoft, ORCID, …) as collaborators
    ask. No code change needed — admin task in Keycloak.

### API track

B0. Write OpenAPI spec covering the *current* `/job/...` and
    `/jobs/...` surface as a baseline (already partially captured in
    `API.md` / `openapi.yaml` skeleton).

B1. Implement `/api/v2/` alongside the legacy routes. The auth
    middleware from track A handles both token types.

B2. Port bakeR's `gateaux_*` helpers to the v2 endpoints; the
    `baker.r` / `joblist.r` we use in psc-estimation switch over.

B3. Emit a `Deprecation` header on legacy responses with a sunset
    date. Six months later, retire.

Most of the v2 surface is already supported by the data model in
`~/dragonfly/gorbachevd` — the work is route bindings, the Keycloak
integration described in §§13–19, and an auth middleware that accepts
service-account tokens via Bearer.

---

## Open questions

- Should `tag` be a single string (current) or a list of labels (Kubernetes-style)?
- Per-job resource overrides — do we want a `cpus_max` cap per report to
  prevent a runaway submitter consuming the cluster?
- Output retention policy: surface as `expires_at` on each output file?
- Should the SSE log endpoint trim very-noisy build output (e.g. apt
  Docker pull lines)?
